// ==WindhawkMod==
// @id windows-10-explorer-restorer
// @name Windows 10 Explorer Restorer
// @description This mod restores the legacy Windows 10 taskbar and styles Start, Search, jump lists, selected shell flyouts and an optional Action Center fallback on Windows 11. Native feature availability depends on installed components.
// @version 1.0.0
// @author babamohammed
// @github https://github.com/babamohammed2022
// @license GPL-3.0
// @architecture x86-64
// @include userinit.exe
// @include explorer.exe
// @include StartMenuExperienceHost.exe
// @include SearchHost.exe
// @include SearchApp.exe
// @include ShellExperienceHost.exe
// @include ShellHost.exe
// @include control.exe
// @compilerOptions -lwininet -lwintrust -lcrypt32 -lbcrypt -ladvapi32 -lgdi32 -luser32 -lshell32 -lole32 -lcomctl32 -lwlanapi -loleaut32 -lruntimeobject -lshcore -ldwmapi -lversion -luuid
// ==/WindhawkMod==

// ==WindhawkModReadme==
/*
# Windows 10 Explorer Restorer

This mod restores the Windows 10 taskbar and a Windows 10 style Start, Search, jump lists, selected shell flyouts and Action Center on Windows 11.

This mod is a best-effort restoration: it uses the Windows 10 components that are still available on the machine, so the result is close to Windows 10 rather than identical to it.

## What the mod does

- **Windows 10 taskbar.** Restores the legacy taskbar through the supplied component, including its private Explorer shell, the notification area, the clock context menu and the verified file downloads it needs.
- **Running UWP apps on the taskbar (experimental, off by default).** UWP apps open and work as usual, they simply have no button on the restored taskbar. The button can be switched on with the `fixUwpTaskbar` setting, and it is off because on the machine this was developed on the trade is bad: the button appears, but the app window comes up empty and the app cannot be opened. Everything that is known is written in the mod source, in the section `UWP APPS ON THE TASKBAR` (the frame is created, is visible, is not cloaked, is positioned on the screen, its app window is attached to it - and it still draws nothing, because the app view is never presented to a Windows 10 shell on Windows 11 24H2). Help on that specific point is welcome.
- **Start and Search.** Applies the Windows 10 look to Start and Search, with a selectable Start menu position (left, centre, right) and a light or dark appearance.
- **Action / Notification Center.** Adds a styled fallback with a localized title, one continuous background, right-aligned Collapse/Expand buttons, nine action shortcuts and a position above the taskbar.
- **Jump lists.** Keeps the 10JumpLists styling and the Windows 10 jump-list behaviour.
- **Shell flyouts.** Adds a separate Windows 10-inspired visual skin for the shell-hosted network/quick-action, clock, volume and battery flyouts. Only targeted surface, border and interaction-state appearance changes; commands, control availability and panel placement stay native. `shellFlyouts` can switch this skin off.
- **Win+X (power user menu).** Provides the Windows 10 menu as a recreation built from Windows 10 reference screenshots, so it is very similar to the original: the same entries in the same order, the same wording in fifteen languages (Italian, English, French, Spanish, German, Portuguese, Dutch, Russian, Japanese, Polish, Danish, Swedish, Norwegian, Finnish and Turkish), the same two separators, 32 px rows, light and dark colours and a single chevron on the submenu. It opens in the Windows 10 position - its bottom-left corner on the top edge of the taskbar, at the left edge of the Start button, never at the pointer - on every Win+X; a second Win+X closes it. A right-click on Start opens the same menu.
- **Guarded menu entries.** Every entry of that menu runs on a guarded worker thread with a time limit: a slow or failing entry is logged and contained instead of hanging or crashing the shell.
- **Anti-crash guard (VEH).** This mod installs a vectored exception handler (`AddVectoredExceptionHandler`) in every process it enters, as the first thing it does, and arms it around its own work: access violations (0xC0000005), illegal instructions and divisions by zero that happen inside a guarded section are contained - the operation is cancelled, the fault is written to the log with the name of the module that faulted and the shell stays alive. It is not SEH: the guard restores the processor context of the guarded section (`RtlCaptureContext`) and continues there, so no `__try/__except` and no `longjmp` inside an exception handler are used.
- **Defence against other mods.** The same section contains a fault that comes from another Windhawk mod's module while this mod is running its own operation, because the handler never looks at who caused the fault. The log line names the module (`... in <module>+0x<offset>`), says whether it is this mod, another Windhawk mod or a system module, and the list of the other Windhawk mods loaded in the process is written once at start-up.
- **Control Panel applet host.** `control.exe` - the process that hosts `appwiz.cpl` and the other applets started from Win+R or from a menu entry - is protected in the same way: the mod attaches to it only for this purpose (no hook of its own, nothing on screen), arms the guard around property sheets, modal and modeless dialogs, and Unicode/ANSI message-loop and dialog-message dispatch paths. It closes the affected applet if a contained fault happens instead of losing the whole process. The list of host processes is the `guardHosts` setting, and `control.exe` is part of the mod's process list (`@include`): that is what actually lets the guard be installed there.
- **C++ exceptions in applet APIs.** C++ exceptions crossing a guarded Control Panel API are caught at that boundary, logged as `[applet-guard] C++ exception crossed ...`, and the dialog/message returns a safe failure value. The RAII crash scope is unwound normally. This does not identify the throwing module and does not catch access violations or other SEH faults; those remain the CrashGuard's separate, limited path.
- **Recovery.** Adds the shell watchdog and the emergency shortcut **Ctrl+Alt+Shift+R**, which brings the native shell back.

## What the mod does not do

- It does not install a complete Windows 10 shell: it reuses the Windows 10 components present on the machine, so some Windows 11 surfaces cannot be made to look exactly like Windows 10.
- It does not behave identically on every Windows 11 build. Native ribbon, Alt+Tab and quick-action paths depend on the build, on the available symbols and on the installed components.
- It does not recreate missing Windows 10 Action Center components. The styled Action Center is a fallback; leave it off when the original center works.
- It does not implement the Windows 7 "Taskbar and Start Menu Properties" dialog.
- It does not bundle ExplorerPatcher and does not reproduce its source code, comments or identifiers; the few techniques shared with that project are reimplemented independently.
- It does not support a rotated display or a taskbar that is not at the bottom.
- **The Win+X menu has no Search entry.** Windows Search is opened with Win+S, with the Search box on the taskbar or from Start.
- It is not registry-write-free: existing per-user shell, recovery, menu and optional tray-state mechanisms remain documented below. `DisableNotificationCenter` and `EnableAutoTray` are no longer written by the normal path: while the mod is active, they are supplied only as process-local registry-read results.

## Known limitations

- **Screen rotation and side taskbars.** The restored shell and the added panels do not re-layout when the display is rotated or when the taskbar is moved to a side. Sign out and back in after such a change.
- **UWP taskbar support.** Experimental and off by default (`fixUwpTaskbar`). When it is on it is a best-effort path over already-running UWP window frames, not pinned shortcuts or a replacement UWP runtime, and it is only installed for recognised private Explorer builds whose function prologue passes validation; otherwise the original taskbar path is kept. The known cost of switching it on: the button appears, the app window stays empty and the app does not open. With it off, UWP apps behave exactly as they do without the mod - they only have no taskbar button.
- **Network icon.** On some machines the native network icon never appears on the restored taskbar, even when the shell reports success. The mod publishes and verifies the icon itself; when the click cannot be routed to the real network flyout it writes a log line and opens no substitute page.
- **Flyout templates.** Windows updates can rename or restructure XAML elements. A selector that no longer matches is skipped; set `shellFlyouts` to `None` to return all selected shell flyout targets to the native appearance.
- **Search visibility.** Turning the Search box on or off may take several minutes to appear on some systems.
- **Action Center animation.** The slide animation is derived from the current panel geometry at the first open. The closing slide-out always plays to completion before the panel is hidden: redundant shell cloaks, shell repositionings and the shortened closing timer are kept from cutting it halfway, so the panel no longer freezes mid-close. When the hook or the worker is not available, the native transition is used.
- **Logs.** Detailed traces are off by default. Errors, hook failures and shell state are always logged; turn on `verboseDiagnostics` only when a detailed trace is needed.
- **Testing.** The existing mod has been exercised on real x64 hardware, but this new flyout skin has not yet been visually verified on your Windows build and the mod has not been tested in a virtual machine. Enable it, sign out and back in, then test Start, Win+X, Search, jump lists, shell flyouts and Explorer windows. Keep a recovery route available: **Ctrl+Alt+Shift+R**, or `C:\Windows\explorer.exe` from Task Manager if the desktop does not come back.

## Registry behaviour and incompatible mods

- **Read-only notification fallback.** When the verified notification compatibility hook is unavailable, `DisableNotificationCenter=1` is virtual only in the private legacy Explorer and in the shell hosts that can create the Action Center. The mod intercepts only `RegQueryValueExW` and `RegGetValueW`; code that reads through another API, native registry APIs, or a cached value sees the real registry value.
- **Read-only tray escalation.** During a forced-network-icon escalation, `EnableAutoTray=0` is likewise virtual only in the private legacy Explorer and only through those two read APIs. The real value is not backed up or overwritten, and removing the virtual value brings the overflow chevron back.
- **Older releases.** A real `DisableNotificationCenter` value left by an older release is deliberately not deleted or changed; remove it manually if it is no longer wanted. At first run this version can perform one compatibility migration for an old `EnableAutoTrayOriginal` backup: it restores the real `EnableAutoTray` value and removes that legacy backup, but creates no new backup.
- **Integrated Anixx mods.** This mod incorporates the functionality of `win10-taskbar-on-win11-24h2`, `fake-explorer-path`, and `win10-taskbar-context-menu-fix-24h2`. Do **not** enable this mod together with any of those three standalone mods. The start-up warning is emitted only when one of those exact identifiers is exposed as a loaded module name; generic `.whl` runtime files are not guessed.

## Crashes and other mods

- **A fault in another mod.** Windhawk loads every enabled mod into the same shell processes. If another mod faults inside `explorer.exe`, `ShellExperienceHost.exe` or `control.exe`, the whole process falls and the shell looks like it crashed, even though this mod did nothing wrong. This mod cannot patch or unload another mod - and does not try to: it contains the fault while one of its own operations or one of the applet paths is running, so the process survives.
- **What the log says.** `[crash-guard] <operation>: violation 0x00000005 contained in <module>+0x<offset> (another Windhawk mod) - the operation was cancelled and the shell keeps running`. The module name and the offset are the same information Windows Error Reporting shows, so the faulting mod can be identified from a single log line.
- **What is not covered.** A fault that happens outside every guarded section, or in a process into which this mod is not loaded, still reaches Windows. An applet host is protected only when the mod is loaded into it: it must appear both in the mod's process list (`control.exe` does) and in `guardHosts`, so another host such as `mmc.exe` needs the source include as well. A contained fault ends the operation, not the code path: it is a repair, not a fix of the other mod.
- **No SEH.** The guard is a VEH with a processor-context restore, not `__try/__except`, because SEH is not available in the Windhawk build environment. C++ exceptions are never intercepted: they still reach their `try/catch`.

## Installation

1. Save your work and note the settings of the shell mods that are enabled now: settings are not migrated between mod IDs.
2. Return the old taskbar mod to the native Windows 11 shell, then disable it before enabling this one. In particular, do not enable `win10-taskbar-on-win11-24h2`, `fake-explorer-path`, or `win10-taskbar-context-menu-fix-24h2` alongside this integrated mod.
3. In Windhawk, create the mod, paste the source, compile it and review the settings.
4. `LiveSwitch` keeps its default "Windows 10 now", so enabling the mod can switch the shell immediately. Keep the current shell build unless another one is wanted deliberately.
5. The component data path is `C:\ProgramData\Win10Shell`; no other mod or tool should manage that directory at the same time. Internet access is required the first time, when the legacy files are prepared.
6. Sign out and back in after the first activation, then test the shell and the panels listed above.

## Main settings

- `winXMenu` - who owns Win+X: `mod` (default, the recreated menu described above), `custom` (the mod's menu only when the native menu does not appear) or `native` (no input hook).
- `winXMenuAnchor`, `winXMenuOffsetX`, `winXMenuOffsetY` - where that menu opens (Start button, or the legacy pointer position) and a pixel adjustment of the result.
- `themeScheme` - `Auto` (follow the Windows app theme), `Light` or `Dark`; it drives the Win+X menu, Start, Search and the panels.
- `startMenuPosition` - left, centre or right.
- `shellOpGuardTimeoutMs` - time limit in milliseconds for the guarded shell operations (1500 by default).
- `guardHosts` - processes that are protected as applet hosts: they get the anti-crash guard only, with no hook of the mod (`control.exe` by default).
- `actionCenterSkin` - the styled Action Center fallback; disable it when the original center works.
- `verboseDiagnostics` - detailed XAML and property traces, off by default.
- `shellFlyouts` - the Windows 10-like styling for shell-hosted network/quick-action, clock, volume and battery flyouts; `None` restores their native XAML styling.
- `forceNetworkTrayDelaySec`, `networkClickFix`, `trayRestoreOverflowChevron` - the tray and network icon options inherited from the taskbar component.

The settings page lists every option with its own description. Changes take effect through the shell services loop; some of them need a sign-out.

## Log lines to check

The prefix tells which part wrote a line: `[winx]` for the Win+X menu (anchor, selected entry), `[menu]` for the immersive menus (theme, colours), `[shell-guard]` for the guarded shell operations, `[tray-force]` for the forced network icon, `[shell-flyouts]` for the native flyout style rules, `[actioncenter-animation]` for the panel animation, `[crash-guard]` for the anti-crash guard (contained faults, faulting module, other Windhawk mods) and `[applet-guard]` for the Control Panel applet host. When something does not work, the line with one of these prefixes usually says why. Every log line is in English: the log is meant to be pasted into a report as it is.

## Credits

- **Anixx** - original Windows 10 taskbar restoration, taskbar context-menu and legacy tray input-indicator fixes. Their `win10-taskbar-on-win11-24h2`, `fake-explorer-path`, and `win10-taskbar-context-menu-fix-24h2` functionality is integrated here; do not enable the standalone mods together with this one.
- **ilovethisgame** - notification crash fix.
- **m417z** - Windhawk, winbindex, code review, Windows 11 Start Menu Styler and Notification Center Styler engines, Shell Flyout Positions, and explorer-frame-classic ribbon/COM-gate work.
- **BBmaster123**, **Sand216** and the styling-guide contributors - Windows10 theme, Unified theme and 10JumpLists references.
- **10Flyouts v4.5 (provided style export)** - visual reference for selected flyout targets; the styling is re-expressed with restrained opacity adjustments, and behavior-changing rules are deliberately excluded.
- **valinet and Amrsatrio** - ExplorerPatcher, credited for the techniques behind the network flyout tiles and the native Alt+Tab routing; both are reimplemented here.
- **AdministratoX** - testing, suggestions, feedback and research, Action Center entrance-animation
- **ac** - supervision and testing.

### Original projects and references

- https://windhawk.net/mods/windows-11-start-menu-styler
- https://windhawk.net/mods/windows-11-notification-center-styler
- https://windhawk.net/mods/shell-flyout-positions
- https://windhawk.net/mods/explorer-frame-classic
- https://github.com/ramensoftware/windows-11-start-menu-styling-guide/tree/main/Themes/Windows10
- https://github.com/ramensoftware/windows-11-notification-center-styling-guide/tree/main/Themes/10JumpLists
- https://github.com/ramensoftware/windows-11-notification-center-styling-guide/tree/main/Themes/Unified
- https://github.com/valinet/ExplorerPatcher - technique-level reference only. No source code, comments, identifiers or byte tables from that project are reproduced in this file.

Credits for the supplied example project do not imply that its authors tested or endorsed this combined version.

### Licence

This mod is published under **GPL-3.0**. The components it merges keep their own terms, all of which are compatible with that choice:

| Component | Author | Licence as published |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Windows 11 Start Menu Styler, Notification Center Styler, explorer-frame-classic, Shell Flyout Positions | m417z | GPL-3.0 (declared in the mod source) |
| Windows 10 taskbar on Windows 11 24H2 or 25H2, Fake Explorer path | Anixx | MIT (no licence declared; the Windhawk mods repository submits undeclared mods under MIT) |
| Fix 11's notifications crash in Windows 10 taskbar | ilovethisgame | MIT (declared) |
| Windows10 theme, 10JumpLists / Unified themes | BBmaster123, Sand216 and the styling-guide contributors | as published in the styling guides |
| ExplorerPatcher | valinet, Amrsatrio | GPL-2.0 - technique reference only; no GPL-2.0 code is reproduced here, so no GPL-2.0/GPL-3.0 combination arises |

The byte-level signatures used to patch `Windows.UI.QuickActions.dll` and `NetworkUX.dll` are written as annotated instruction descriptors, one entry per byte, and are re-validated at run time before any write: they describe instruction sequences of Microsoft's own modules and were derived from that encoding, not transcribed from another project. If you maintain one of the projects listed above and believe something here goes beyond a technique, open an issue and it will be removed or re-licensed.
*/
// ==/WindhawkModReadme==

// ==WindhawkModSettings==
/*
- LiveSwitch: to-legacy
  $name: Shell to use now
  $description: >-
    This setting switches the shell right now: to-legacy = switch to the Windows 10 shell immediately
    (default: after installing the mod the taskbar comes back without signing out and in again), to-win11 =
    switch back to the Windows 11 shell right now, none = no switch now and the Windows 10 shell is used at
    the next logon. Everything else is automatic. Do not enable this integrated mod together with Anixx's
    win10-taskbar-on-win11-24h2, fake-explorer-path or win10-taskbar-context-menu-fix-24h2 mods.
  $options:
  - to-legacy: Windows 10 (now)
  - to-win11: Windows 11 (now)
  - none: no switch now
- ShellBuild: anixx
  $name: Windows 10 shell build to use
  $description: 'This setting picks the Windows 10 shell copy to use: anixx = 10.0.19039.1, the copy used by Anixx''s mod
    (the only build with a verified notification-crash-fix offset; preferred for the classic theme) and
    recente = 10.0.19041.7725 (LTSC 2021 updated to September 2026), newer and without a verified offset, so
    notification suppression is used as a fallback. The choice takes effect the next time the Windows 10
    shell starts.'
  $options:
  - anixx: 10.0.19039.1 (Anixx build)
  - recente: 10.0.19041.7725 (LTSC 2021)
- Win10ActionCenter: true
  $name: Action Center - Virtual UseLiteLayout
  $description: 'This setting exposes UseLiteLayout=1 within the process without writing to HKLM. It does not guarantee
    the complete Windows 10 Action Center on 24H2 or later and does not disable the notification
    crash-prevention fallback. Restart the host processes or sign out and back in.'
- AltTabStyle: win10
  $name: Native Alt+Tab style
  $description: 'This setting selects the Alt+Tab host. Windows 10 requires the original hosts and compatible symbols: if
    they are unavailable the mod reports it in the log and keeps the system behaviour, with no replacement
    UI. Windows 11 uses the original XAML path. Restart Explorer to clear cached UI.'
  $options:
  - win10: Windows 10 (native, if available)
  - win11: Windows 11 (system)
- winXMenu: mod
  $name: Win+X menu
  $description: 'This setting decides who owns Win+X: mod = the mod menu replaces the native one and opens on every
    chord, anchored to the Start button like in Windows 10; native = Win+X and the right-click on Start are
    left to the Windows 10 shell (no input hook at all); custom = the native menu keeps priority and the mod
    menu is shown only if the native one has not appeared within ~350 ms. A change takes effect through the
    shell services loop; sign out and back in if it does not apply.'
  $options:
  - mod: Mod menu (always, replaces the native one)
  - native: Windows 10 (native)
  - custom: Custom menu (fallback, native first)
- winXMenuAnchor: start
  $name: Win+X menu - Anchor
  $description: 'This setting chooses where the Win+X menu is anchored: start = Windows 10 position, the bottom-left
    corner of the menu glued to the top edge of the taskbar at the left edge of the Start button, in the
    same place on every chord whatever the mouse is doing; cursor = legacy behaviour, the menu opens at the
    mouse pointer and can end up on top of the taskbar.'
  $options:
  - start: Start button / taskbar edge (Windows 10)
  - cursor: Mouse pointer (legacy)
- winXMenuOffsetX: 0
  $name: Win+X menu - Horizontal offset (px)
  $description: 'This setting shifts the anchor point sideways, -8 to 64 px. 0 = the left edge of the Start button. Set
    18 to reproduce the 2015 Windows 10 reference screenshot of the power-user menu.'
- winXMenuOffsetY: 0
  $name: Win+X menu - Vertical offset (px)
  $description: 'This setting shifts the anchor point vertically, -64 to 64 px. 0 = the bottom edge of the menu rests
    exactly on the top edge of the taskbar; positive values push the menu down over the taskbar (the 2015
    Windows 10 reference screenshot is +16) and negative values lift it above the taskbar.'
- guardHosts: control.exe
  $name: Crash guard - Applet host processes
  $description: 'This setting lists the processes, separated by commas or semicolons, in which the mod attaches only to
    keep the anti-crash guard alive without installing any visual hook. The default, control.exe, is the
    host of the Control Panel applets started from Win+R or from a menu entry (appwiz.cpl and the other .cpl
    files): another Windhawk mod faulting there takes the applet down, and with this guard the applet closes
    instead of bringing the process with it. Add mmc.exe or rundll32.exe if an applet of yours lives in one
    of them, but a host is protected only when the mod is loaded into it: control.exe is part of the mod
    process list, while another host must be added to the include list of the mod source as well. It takes
    effect the next time those processes start.'
- ExplorerRibbon: win10
  $name: File Explorer interface
  $description: 'This setting selects the File Explorer interface: the Windows 10 ribbon without tabs, or the Windows 11
    command bar. It uses the classicRibbonUI method by m417z, with no Moments variants, and requires the
    Microsoft fallback implementation to still be present. Reopen Explorer windows or restart Explorer.'
  $options:
  - win10: Windows 10 ribbon
  - win11: Windows 11 command bar
- theme: Windows10
  $name: Skin
  $description: 'This setting selects the skin: the Windows 10 skin, ported from the Windows 11 Start Menu Styler by
    m417z with the Windows10 theme by BBmaster123, styles the Start menu and the search menu (Minimal is the
    reduced version) and None turns the skin off.'
  $options:
  - ? ''
    : None
  - Windows10: Windows 10
  - Windows10_variant_Minimal: Windows 10 (Minimal)
- jumpLists: Windows10
  $name: Taskbar jump lists
  $description: 'This setting selects the look of the taskbar jump lists (the menus which open when a taskbar button is
    right-clicked). Windows 10 is the 10JumpLists skin, ported from the Windows 11 Notification Center
    Styler and its styling guide and applied by itself whatever the skin above is set to, because the jump
    lists are drawn by ShellExperienceHost.exe and ShellHost.exe, the two shell processes this mod is also
    loaded into. Choose None to leave the jump lists to Windows (for example if another mod styles them) or
    if the mod is used for the Start menu only.'
  $options:
  - ? ''
    : None
  - Windows10: Windows 10
- shellFlyouts: Windows10
  $name: Native shell flyouts
  $description: 'This applies a scoped Windows 10-inspired visual skin to flyout XAML hosted by
    ShellExperienceHost.exe and ShellHost.exe: network/quick-action tiles, Wi-Fi list and connection states,
    clock, volume and battery surfaces where those templates exist. It follows the system theme and accent.
    No commands, panel positions or fixed dimensions are altered; controls remain native and no functional
    control is hidden. Choose None to leave these targets untouched. Updated Windows templates which do not match a selector are left alone.'
  $options:
  - ? ''
    : None
  - Windows10: Windows 10-like
- actionCenterSkin: win10Unified
  $name: Action Center - Skin
  $description: 'This setting enables the Windows 10-style fallback for Win+N and Win+A, inspired by Unified by Sand216.
    The panels remain separate, jump lists are not changed and None disables only this fallback.'
  $options:
  - win10Unified: Windows 10-like (Unified)
  - ? ''
    : None
- actionCenterScheme: auto
  $name: Action Center - Color scheme
  $description: 'This setting selects the colours of that fallback: Auto follows the native panel theme, while Light and
    Dark apply only to Win+N and Win+A without changing Start or jump lists.'
  $options:
  - auto: Auto
  - light: Light
  - dark: Dark
- actionCenterHideCalendar: false
  $name: Action Center - Hide calendar and Focus
  $description: 'This setting hides the calendar and Focus to more closely resemble Windows 10; when it is off, both
    native features remain available.'
- actionCenterLayout23: compact
  $name: Action Center - Layout
  $description: 'This setting uses a single bottom group containing the nine supported actions. The native grid is hidden
    only after a successful insertion, tablet mode is not available on Windows 11 and the old 4/8/16
    selections and the native four-column layout are no longer used.'
  $options:
  - compact: Single compact group (9 actions)
  - native: Native controls without added tiles (recovery)
- actionCenterHidePanelIcons23: true
  $name: Action Center - Hide panel volume and battery icons
  $description: 'This setting applies to the panel only: it hides the mute button and the battery indicator, keeps the
    volume slider and replaces the output/mixer icon with a chevron when full slider hiding is disabled.
    Compact mode also hides the old footer, replaced by All settings. The taskbar is not changed.'
- actionCenterHideBars24: true
  $name: Action Center - Hide volume, brightness and battery
  $description: 'This setting hides the entire slider group and the battery indicator inside the panel, not on the
    taskbar, and takes precedence over the icons-only option.'
- actionCenterFullHeight24: true
  $name: Action Center - Full height at the right edge
  $description: 'This setting is the Windows 11 fallback that positions the panel above the actual taskbar edge. The
    optional first-open cloak animation derives its destination from live monitor geometry; without a
    qualifying animation target, positioning follows the native transition. Geometry is read without
    changing the taskbar.'
- actionCenterSuppressAnimations25: true
  $name: Notification Center - Request native transition suppression
  $description: 'This setting requests DWM transition suppression only; it does not interrupt the native XAML/composition
    transitions needed for closing and reopening. A separate candidate cloak hook may animate qualifying
    first-open and close transitions in the private Explorer, while unmatched windows keep the native
    behaviour of the build.'
- actionCenterWidth25: 360
  $name: Notification Center - Fixed width (DIP)
  $description: 'This setting fixes a stable logical width, limited to 280-480 DIP. The rectangle is calculated from the
    monitor work area and DPI, not from animated content measurements, and extends down to the taskbar
    without covering it.'
- themeScheme: auto
  $name: Colour scheme
  $description: 'This setting selects which colours the skin uses. With Auto the skin follows the Windows app theme
    (Settings > Personalization > Colors) and switches by itself if you change it while Windows is running.'
  $options:
  - auto: Auto (follow Windows)
  - light: Light
  - dark: Dark
- verboseDiagnostics: false
  $name: Detailed XAML diagnostics
  $description: 'This setting enables per-element XAML/property/JavaScript trace output and the optional
    notification-database schema probe. It is off by default; operational errors, hook failures and
    shell/network state diagnostics remain visible, and notification payload contents are never logged.'
- startMenuPosition: left
  $name: Start menu position
  $description: 'This setting chooses where the Start menu is anchored horizontally: Left is where Windows 10 puts it and
    Windows default leaves the position to Windows.'
  $options:
  - windowsDefault: Windows default
  - left: Left
  - center: Center
  - right: Right
- forceNetworkTrayIcon: true
  $name: Network icon - Force it into the taskbar
  $description: 'This setting forces the network icon into the taskbar: if the native PNI (pnidui.dll) does not register
    the icon within the delay below, the mod registers it with Shell_NotifyIconW, with its own owner window,
    the system network GUID, the authentic icon taken from the verified pnidui.dll and the real state from
    the Network List Manager. The icon is withdrawn automatically when the native PNI registers. The left
    click opens the authentic Windows 10 network flyout through the shell factory (experience manager
    NetworkFlyout) and the right click shows the two authentic entries of the pnidui menu.
    ms-availablenetworks: is never used as a substitute.'
- forceNetworkTrayDelaySec: 12
  $name: Network icon - Wait before the mod intervenes (s)
  $description: 'This setting is the number of seconds to wait, from the activation of the mod taskbar, before the forced
    registration takes the place of the native one (0-600).'
- forceNetworkTrayResetTraySettings: false
  $name: Network icon - Reset the taskbar state once
  $description: 'This setting saves %ProgramData%\Win10Shell\tray-state-backup.bin and then deletes
    HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\TrayNotify (IconStreams, PastIconsStream): that
    binary state is shared between the Windows 11 shell and the private Windows 10 one and can keep the icon
    marked as hidden. It is done once only (marker tray-state-reset.done). Custom icon positions are lost:
    the backup is there to restore them.'
- trayRestoreOverflowChevron: true
  $name: Network icon - Restore the taskbar overflow button
  $description: 'This setting puts the overflow button back by removing the private, in-memory EnableAutoTray=0 answer
    once the forced icon is stable. The real registry value is never backed up or overwritten. The override is
    visible only to the private Explorer through RegQueryValueExW and RegGetValueW; if the icon later disappears,
    the private read override is enabled again and the log says so.'
- forceNetworkTrayAction: auto
  $name: Network icon - Manual action
  $description: 'This setting selects the manual action: auto = the intervention ladder decides; reinstall-now = at the
    next settings application the forced icon is removed and registered again straight away, which is useful
    to test the path without restarting the shell.'
  $options:
  - auto: Auto
  - reinstall-now: Reinstall now
- actionCenterAnimation: true
  $name: Action Center - Open/close animation
  $description: 'This setting enables the Windows 10-style sideways movement of the panel with a DWMWA_CLOAK hook,
    installed in explorer.exe, ShellExperienceHost.exe and ShellHost.exe, because the panel belongs to one
    of these processes and not to the private Explorer: that was the reason the animation never started.'
- actionCenterAnimInMs: 220
  $name: Action Center - Opening duration (ms)
  $description: 'This setting is the opening duration in milliseconds: 0 = no movement when opening and the native
    transition stays (40-600).'
- actionCenterAnimOutMs: 140
  $name: Action Center - Closing duration (ms)
  $description: 'This setting is the closing duration in milliseconds: 0 = no movement when closing (40-600).'
- actionCenterCloseDelayMs: 50
  $name: Action Center - Closing delay (ms)
  $description: 'This setting replaces the ~2000 ms timer of the panel (1-1900), only with the panel open and only with
    twinui.pcshell.dll on the call stack.'
- shellOpGuardTimeoutMs: 1500
  $name: 'shell::: guard - Maximum wait (ms)'
  $description: 'This setting is the maximum wait in milliseconds for the operations that open a shell namespace, for
    example "Customize notification area icons": they run on a service thread with this time cap, and if the
    shell does not answer the interface does not hang and only the log is written (200-10000).'
- fixUwpTaskbar: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar (experimental)'
  $description: >-
    EXPERIMENTAL, OFF BY DEFAULT. When it is on, UWP apps (Settings, Calculator, Store, ...) get a button on
    the Windows 10 taskbar. On the machine this was developed on the trade is a bad one: the button appears,
    but the app window comes up with no content and the app cannot be opened, which is worse than having no
    button at all. Everything that is known about it is written in the mod source, in the "UWP APPS ON THE
    TASKBAR" section: the frame is created, is visible, is not cloaked, sits at the right place on the screen
    and its app window is attached to it - and it still draws nothing, because the app view is never presented
    to a Windows 10 shell running on Windows 11 24H2. Leave this off unless you are working on that problem.
    The other uwpTaskbar* options only have an effect when this one is true. Sign out and back in after a
    change.
- uwpTaskbarNoShellManagedHook: true
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not hook user32!IsShellManagedWindow'
  $description: >-
    The mod also hooks user32!IsShellManagedWindow (ordinal 2574) inside the shell, so that the taskbar
    treats an ApplicationFrameWindow (Settings, Calculator, Store, ...) as a normal window. The hook is
    installed only after a sanity check, and from v14 the changed answer goes only to the taskbar code:
    every other module loaded in the shell keeps getting the true answer, because the service that attaches
    the app view to the frame needs it and lying to it leaves the app window empty. Set this to true to rely
    only on the window property Microsoft.Windows.ShellManagedWindowAsNormalWindow and on the AppUserModel ID
    (which from v15 is really written on the frame: the IID of IPropertyStore was mistyped and the whole
    AppUserModel ID chain had never worked). From v19 this is true by default: the hook is off, that is the A/B test. To switch the hook back on set uwpTaskbarShellManagedHook to true, which has priority over this key. If the UWP windows open empty, try this option first. Sign out
    and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarShellManagedHook: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Hook user32!IsShellManagedWindow (A/B test)'
  $description: >-
    From v19 the hook of user32!IsShellManagedWindow (ordinal 2574) inside the shell is OFF by default.
    This is the A/B test: the hook makes the taskbar accept an ApplicationFrameWindow (Settings, Calculator,
    Store, ...) by telling it "this frame is not shell managed", and that answer is the only place where this
    mod alters what Windows says. With the hook off, every module loaded in the shell gets the true answer
    again. If the UWP windows then show their content - even without a taskbar button - the empty window was
    caused by that altered answer and the fix belongs there. If they stay empty, the fault is upstream: the
    24H2 shell service never presents the app view to a Windows 10 shell, and hooking or not hooking changes
    nothing. This key has priority over uwpTaskbarNoShellManagedHook. Sign out and back in after a
    change.
- uwpTaskbarAddMethod: auto
  $name: UWP apps on the taskbar - How the button is added
  $description: >-
    auto = ITaskbarList::AddTab first and, if the taskbar removes the button straight away, the SHELLHOOK /
    HSHELL_WINDOWCREATED notification to the taskbar window; taskbarList = only the documented ITaskbarList
    API; shellHook = only the SHELLHOOK notification to the taskband; shellHookTray = the SHELLHOOK
    notification to Shell_TrayWnd. Sign out and back in after a change.
  $options:
  - auto: Automatic (recommended)
  - taskbarList: ITaskbarList::AddTab
  - shellHook: SHELLHOOK to the taskband
  - shellHookTray: SHELLHOOK to Shell_TrayWnd
- uwpTaskbarNoModernFilterFix: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not touch the modern-window filter property'
  $description: >-
    Explorer only gives a taskbar button to a "modern" window (the frames marked with the numeric window
    property 0xA920) when its AppUserModel ID resolves to an item of the AppsFolder. On a Windows 10
    Explorer running on Windows 11 24H2 that resolution can fail, and then the button is never created,
    whatever else is done. By default the mod removes that property from the frame and makes sure the
    AppUserModel ID is present, so the window is judged with the normal window rules; the log line of
    every frame tells what was found. Set this to true to leave Windows untouched and rely only on the
    other paths. Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarNoStyleFix: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not correct the extended window styles'
  $description: >-
    Documented rule: a window with WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW never gets a button, and a window with an owner only
    gets one when it has WS_EX_APPWINDOW. By default the mod clears the first and sets the second on the
    frames that need it. Set this to true to leave the styles alone. Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarBandFix: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Move the frame to the desktop z-order band'
  $description: >-
    Experimental. Every window lives in a z-order band (ZBID) and the taskbar only accepts the windows
    that are in a valid band. If the log shows "banda=" with a value other than 0 or 1 for a frame and
    no method works, set this to true: the mod moves the frame to the desktop band with
    user32!SetWindowBand before creating the button. It can change the z-order of the UWP window.
- uwpTaskbarNoSymbolProbe: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not list the Explorer symbols'
  $description: >-
    At startup the mod writes in the log the Explorer symbols whose name mentions the taskbar (CTaskList,
    CTaskBand, IsValidTabWindowForTray, ...): that is the evidence the diagnosis is based on, and it costs
    a symbol download the first time. Set this to true to skip it.
- uwpTaskbarRequireAppWindow: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Require a visible app window inside the frame'
  $description: >-
    OFF by default. On Windows 11 24H2 the XAML content of a UWP app is not always a visible
    "Windows.UI.Core.CoreWindow" child (the log showed contenuto=no for Settings), so requiring it would
    exclude the real apps as well. Turn this on only if the frame lines in the log show contenuto=si for
    the apps and contenuto=no for the ghost buttons. Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarSalvataggio: true
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Attach the app window to the empty frame'
  $description: >-
    ON by default, because this is the fix. The log showed that the app window exists and draws, but stays
    a top level window instead of sitting inside the frame: ApplicationFrameWindow (ApplicationFrameHost) on
    one side, Windows.UI.Core.CoreWindow of the app (SystemSettings, Calculator, ...) on the other, with the
    same title. With this option the mod matches them by title and class, sets WS_CHILD / clears WS_POPUP and
    calls SetParent, exactly as the documentation of SetParent prescribes for a window that was a child of the
    desktop, then moves the content over the client area of the frame. From v17 it also aligns the z-order band
    (ZBID) of the two windows, because DWM composes each band separately and a child in a different band from
    its parent is not drawn inside it, and it verifies after a couple of seconds that the content is still there
    (retrying with the opposite band strategy if something undoes the work). Documented side effect: a cross
    process SetParent between different DPI awareness modes resets the DPI awareness of the child process. The
    work
    runs on the helper thread, never while holding the lock and never on the user interface thread of the
    shell, so a blocked window cannot freeze Explorer. Set this to false to leave the two windows apart.
    Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarNoGraft: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not attach the app window to the frame'
  $description: >-
    From v16 the mod attaches the app window (a Windows.UI.Core.CoreWindow) to the ApplicationFrameWindow
    with SetParent, because on this system the frame arrives without its content. It is the second suspect:
    SetParent across two processes resets the DPI awareness of the child process, and that can stop the app
    window from drawing. Leave this at false. Set it to true only if the A/B test on
    uwpTaskbarShellManagedHook showed that the empty window is not caused by the IsShellManagedWindow hook:
    with this at true the mod no longer moves any window, and if the app then draws, the graft is the problem.
    Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarNoCallerFilter: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Tell every caller that the frames are not shell managed (old behaviour)'
  $description: >-
    OFF by default, and this is the setting that matters most. The mod answers "not shell managed" only to
    the taskbar code (the calls that come from Explorer itself); every other module loaded in the shell
    keeps getting the true answer, because the service that attaches the app view to the frame needs it
    and lying to it leaves the app window empty. Set this to true to go back to the old behaviour, where
    the answer was changed for every caller: use it only if the buttons stop appearing, and tell it in
    the log. Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarActivationHooks: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Repair the activation of the button (off by default)'
  $description: >-
    OFF by default, and deliberately so. When this is true the mod hooks, inside the shell and for UWP
    frames only, the documented user32 activation functions (SetForegroundWindow, SwitchToThisWindow,
    ShowWindow, BringWindowToTop, SetActiveWindow): it writes in the log what the taskbar does when the
    button is clicked and, when the click does not bring the app forward, it retries in the documented
    ways and as a last resort relaunches the app by AppUserModel ID with IApplicationActivationManager.
    Those hooks run on the user interface thread of the shell: on this machine they made the Windows 10
    shell die a few seconds after the switch, so from this version they have to be asked for explicitly.
    Turn this on only to take the diagnosis, then turn it off again.
- uwpTaskbarNoActivationHooks: true
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not repair the activation of the button (old switch)'
  $description: >-
    Kept for compatibility with the settings saved by the previous versions. The activation hooks are now
    off unless uwpTaskbarActivationHooks is true, so this switch only has to stay true. Setting it to
    false does not turn the hooks back on.
- uwpTaskbarNoSystemFilter: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Give a button to the system surfaces too'
  $description: >-
    The on-screen keyboard, the handwriting panel, the emoji picker, the clipboard and the shell hosts are
    real UWP apps with a real ApplicationFrameWindow: the taskbar can give them a button, and that is the
    ghost button with empty thumbnails. By default the mod never gives a button to those AppUserModel IDs
    and removes the one left behind by the startup enumeration. Set this to true to treat them like any
    other app. Sign out and back in after a change.
- uwpTaskbarConservative: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Conservative mode (only the safe APIs)'
  $description: >-
    Emergency switch. When this is true the mod does only what the v3 did - mark the frame as a normal
    window and add or remove the button with ITaskbarList::AddTab / DeleteTab and the SHELLHOOK
    notification - and turns off everything else: no activation hooks, no window property store, no style
    or identity corrections, no symbol listing. From this version the mod also sets this mode by itself,
    without asking, if the Windows 10 shell dies a few seconds after the switch.
- uwpTaskbarNoLogFile: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not write the log file'
  $description: >-
    Besides the Windhawk log, the mod writes every diagnostic line to %TEMP%\\uwp-taskbar.log (UTF-16,
    with a timestamp), so the diagnosis does not depend on finding the Windhawk log window. Set this to
    true to keep only the Windhawk log.
- uwpTaskbarNoClickWatch: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not watch the clicks on the taskbar'
  $description: >-
    On this machine a UWP frame can be "visible" for Win32 and still be unusable: empty (no XAML content
    inside the frame), minimized, on another virtual desktop or outside the screen. From this version the
    mod notices it: it watches the clicks on the taskbar with a low-level mouse hook (no code is injected
    into the user interface thread of the shell) and, when a click brings nothing forward, it recomposes
    the state of that window with documented APIs - restore, IVirtualDesktopManager::MoveWindowToDesktop,
    SetForegroundWindow - and as a last relaunches the app by AppUserModel ID, like the Start menu does.
    Set this to true to leave the clicks entirely to Windows.
- uwpTaskbarNoGhostCleanup: false
  $name: 'UWP apps on the taskbar - Do not remove the buttons of the frames that are not eligible'
  $description: >-
    When a frame stops being eligible (the app was closed, cloaked or minimized) the mod removes its
    button. By default it also removes, once, the button of every frame that is not eligible, because the
    startup enumeration of the taskbar can leave behind a button for an app that is not really running.
    Set this to true if you prefer to keep those buttons.
*/
